00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.220
Istio is an open platform to
connect, manage, and secure

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.120
services.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.310
That's what you would
read, even if you

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.440
go to our website, istio.io.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.640
Essentially, it's an
open service mesh,

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.810
where we would like developers
and operators to not

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:07.050
have headaches about
how to connect services,

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:11.000
how to think about making them
resilient, how to secure them,

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.580
and how to manage the runtime.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.490
We would like Istio
to be able to do

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.610
that for developers
and operators

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.970
across all environments
and clouds.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.970
And when I say "services," it's
really all kinds of services,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.200
not necessarily just micro.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.539
It could be anything
you're building--

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:38.090
MySQL API service, a
really small micro service

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:43.490
within your app for payments or
shopping in any given language.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.660
So Istio takes an
approach of working

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:49.310
with polyglot environments.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:50.870
You know, no matter
what language you

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.740
write your services in and
no matter where you deploy,

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:57.980
Istio would like to give
you a uniform substrate

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.450
between your application
and network, which

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:06.260
can take care of connectivity
between services,

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.000
resiliency between services.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.560
So resiliency includes things
like retries, failover,

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.020
all of the good stuff
and distributed systems.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.460
Securing services.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:17.960
We think internal
services should

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:22.070
be as secure as external
ones, so security by default.

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:25.010
And have complete
observability and visibility

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:30.550
of metrics all the way from L3
to L7 across all your services.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:31.300
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.740
So that sounds like
a great description

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.800
from that point of view
of product manager.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.560
You mentioned, at
the first description

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.740
that you gave of the
whole project set,

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.230
was something about
a service mesh.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.020
What does it mean to
be a service mesh?

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:49.100
What is a mesh?

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.260
VARUN TALWAR: So it's
a fairly new term,

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.150
so it's worth
explaining what that is.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:58.149
Essentially, think
about a layer--

00:09:58.149 --> 00:09:59.690
and some people call
it the L5 layer,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:04.590
which is basically a layer
between your application

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.630
and your network.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:12.240
And when you think about it,
you are basically creating--

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.830
we are injecting a proxy
next to every service.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:20.130
And those are in the data
path of all the service

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:21.990
to service communication.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.170
And they are all
interconnected, and also

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.360
connected to a
common control plane.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.750
And that interconnected set of
proxies which are living next

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:33.090
to every service is
what typically is being

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:34.710
called as the service mesh.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.780
The reason it's
so interesting is

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:40.110
that once you think of
mesh as a layer which

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.920
exists like a network does, you
can, at the application layer,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.300
start to offload things like
connectivity, resiliency,

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.630
security, visibility
to that layer.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:55.380
So historically, you would
do this in either application

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:55.950
libraries.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.040
So if we're building in
Java or Python or Go,

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.430
there's a bunch of libraries
in each of these language

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.070
environments that you would
import and build logic

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:05.970
into those.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.230
And/or you would do L3
level security and policy,

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.650
like IP whitelisting,
firewalling, VPN-ing,

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:15.550
VPC peering, and so on.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:20.250
So we think service mesh
is a space between the two

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.250
which can offload a
few things from L7

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:28.482
and give policy-driven controls
to operate your network.

00:11:28.482 --> 00:11:29.940
MARK MANDEL: So
the service mesh is

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:32.260
kind of the bit that's
between everything.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:35.276
It's kind of like the dark
matter of the universe.

00:11:35.276 --> 00:11:36.090
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:38.114
Haven't described
it that way yet.

00:11:38.114 --> 00:11:39.530
MARK MANDEL: So
surface mesh isn't

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.654
all the different services
that talk to each other,

00:11:41.654 --> 00:11:43.980
but instead the piece
that sits between them

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.530
and makes sure that everything
goes OK between all of them.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:47.850
Would that be about right?

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:48.600
VARUN TALWAR: Yes.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.760
It's the piece that makes
sure that, if you think

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.670
all your services as
sort of nodes in a graph,

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.490
mesh is basically making
sure you don't have to think

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.290
about managing the edges.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:01.590
And everything that you have
to do on the edges, which

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:03.880
is, how do I connect them?

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.490
How do I retry my
connections there?

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.340
How do I load balance
my connections there?

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:09.930
How do I apply policy
on my connections?

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:13.184
So that's one way to
understand what the mesh is.

00:12:13.184 --> 00:12:14.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.860
So we're talking a
little bit about level 7,

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.840
level 3, level 5.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.760
Just a little bit
for context, I guess,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.730
it's worth mentioning that
I'm assuming you're referring

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:29.910
to the OSI levels of networking,
where level 3 is networking,

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.060
level 7 is application.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.310
VARUN TALWAR: That is correct.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:34.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What does
it mean level 5 for you?

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.000
What does it actually
mean for you?

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.730
According to this, it
means session, which

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.489
is kind of an ambiguous term.

00:12:40.489 --> 00:12:41.280
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.220
Classic OSI stack is
what I'm referring to.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:45.920
You know, L3 is TCP.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.150
L4 is HTTP.

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.991
GRPC also in a similar bucket.

00:12:50.991 --> 00:12:51.490
So yeah.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:55.320
I am saying, I guess,
things above HTTP layer.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.500
Again, L5 is sort of
just a description

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.310
to say it's above the network
and below the application.

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:05.190
I don't think it's a formal
concept in the OSI stack.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:06.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
then from the point

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:07.404
of view of a
developer, basically

00:13:07.404 --> 00:13:11.360
it's like, until so far, let's
say that you have two services

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.947
and that they're running
on Google Cloud Platform

00:13:13.947 --> 00:13:15.280
and Compute Engine or something.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:16.770
You have two services.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.620
And you want to do
an HTTP request.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.870
One of the services is
exposing a REST API.

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:25.710
So what you normally do is
you just send an HTTP request.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.660
So HTTP woudl be the transport.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.810
Network would be TCP,
whatever goes below.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:31.620
We don't care.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:34.540
How does Istio change this?

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.230
What is the change in
the whole thing that

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:40.920
allows us to have all the
security and monitoring,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.900
and all that good stuff
you were talking about?

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.400
VARUN TALWAR:
Essentially, service A

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.430
talking to service B in a
Kubernetes environment, which

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:50.280
is what we have
prioritized Istio today,

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.250
but we would hope to take it
to other environments soon.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:55.590
You would basically
install Istio.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:59.760
And we would transparently
intercept the A to B traffic.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.270
We would deploy what we call
sidecar proxies, or basically,

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:06.300
proxies next to A and B,
just to keep it simple.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:12.390
And all A to B communication
then becomes the local proxy A

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:14.250
to local proxy B communication.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.700
And once you do
that, and once Istio

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.190
has intercepted the
traffic, the proxies

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:25.290
are smart to do resiliency
things like retries,

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.990
circuit breaking, failovers.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:32.130
And once Istio is
intercepting the traffic,

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:34.440
you can apply
policies like, hey,

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:38.130
don't make more than 100
calls per minute from A to B,

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.810
or a rate limit, or a quota.

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.660
You could also do load
balancing between A and B

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.300
without the applications
having to do anything.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.310
You could get all the
telemetry between A and B

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:50.820
in terms of latency, error
rate, QPS between A and B

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:54.570
without the applications
having to import and wire

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:57.840
in monitoring libraries,
which you would typically do.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.460
Sven, you want to
expand on that?

00:14:59.460 --> 00:14:59.790
SVEN MAWSON: Sure.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:00.290
Yeah.

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:03.120
So the way I think
about this is that Istio

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.610
is sort of making your
network a lot smarter.

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.670
So from an application view,
you don't have to know about all

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.310
of the stuff that's going on.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.860
It's just suddenly, your
network now is more resilient

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:17.550
because you get all these
retries and circuit breaking

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.510
and the things Varun
was talking about.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.150
And you get all these policies.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.399
You don't have to actually
go and write any code

00:15:24.399 --> 00:15:25.440
to do any of that, right?

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.600
It's just sort of automatic.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.185
And under the hood, yeah, we're
adding these sidecar proxies

00:15:30.185 --> 00:15:32.777
and we're intercepting traffic.

00:15:32.777 --> 00:15:34.610
But that's sort of the
implementation detail

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.484
that could change in
future versions of this.

00:15:36.484 --> 00:15:39.982
The idea is really just we want
to make the network operate

00:15:39.982 --> 00:15:41.940
at the service level, at
the application level,

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.540
and be able to do a
lot of these things

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:46.910
that people are
having to write custom

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:49.610
code in their
application to do today.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.770
The network should basically
handle that for them.

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:52.900
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.066
And Varun, I mean, you
touched on this a little bit.

00:15:55.066 --> 00:15:57.030
You said there was a
priority for Kubernetes.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.600
I've heard this referred to
as Kubernetes native, which

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:01.550
I kind of think is quite cute.

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.950
Why-- and Sven, you might
answer this best-- why

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:06.410
was that decision made to
prioritize Kubernetes and make

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:07.947
that the base
platform for Istio?

00:16:07.947 --> 00:16:10.280
SVEN MAWSON: So there's sort
of a couple different parts

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:11.460
to that.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:14.100
One is that we were sitting very
close to the Kubernetes folks.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.390
So that made it easy to
talk to them about problems

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:18.230
we were having.

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:21.267
More seriously, Kubernetes
provides a lot of value

00:16:21.267 --> 00:16:22.850
and provides a lot
of services that we

00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:24.016
didn't have to go implement.

00:16:24.016 --> 00:16:26.900
So it provides a service
registration mechanism,

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.990
and orchestration, and
all these other pieces.

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:32.150
It even actually has some
built-in authentication

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:33.740
that we're taking advantage of.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.840
So it really helped us get
to market a lot quicker

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:39.300
with this project because it
had all those pieces we didn't

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:41.190
have to rebuild ourselves.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.270
So that was a big
part of why Kubernetes

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:44.660
was sort of the first target.

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:45.535
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:16:45.535 --> 00:16:47.270
OK, so let's talk
practically, then.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.780
So say I'm a developer.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:50.090
This sounds really cool.

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:51.840
I don't want to write
all this retry logic

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.050
myself or do all this
security stuff myself.

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:56.630
I want to install Istio
and have a play with it.

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:57.930
How do I do that?

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.630
How do I make this thing
work with my applications?

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.504
SVEN MAWSON: If you're
running on Kubernetes,

00:17:02.504 --> 00:17:06.200
you can just go to
istio.io and go to the--

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.932
we have sort of a
tutorial section.

00:17:08.932 --> 00:17:10.890
And one of those tutorials
is Installing Istio.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.442
It takes about 5 to 10 minutes.

00:17:13.442 --> 00:17:14.900
You can install it
in your cluster.

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.802
And then there's a
bunch of actual sort

00:17:16.802 --> 00:17:18.260
of walkthroughs of
different things

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:19.560
you can do with it after that.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:20.768
So you play with the routing.

00:17:20.768 --> 00:17:23.092
You can play with
policy enforcement.

00:17:23.092 --> 00:17:24.800
Authentication actually,
you don't really

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:25.633
have to do anything.

00:17:25.633 --> 00:17:28.707
It just automatically secures
your communication for you.

00:17:28.707 --> 00:17:30.290
But then if you want
to write policies

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:33.860
against who is acting in
your mesh, you can do that.

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.233
So it's actually pretty easy.

00:17:35.233 --> 00:17:37.066
I'm sort of surprised
every time I go and do

00:17:37.066 --> 00:17:39.950
a demo kind of how easy it is
to get started with this thing.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:43.280
It's really, really
just 10 minutes

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:45.230
to get something
useful up and running.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.420
MARK MANDEL: And then if I
want to configure Istio--

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:48.170
like you were
talking about being

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.780
able to set up connectivity
or load balancing-- how do I

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:51.840
go about doing that?

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:52.631
SVEN MAWSON: Right.

00:17:52.631 --> 00:17:56.150
So Istio comes with a command
line tool called Istio Control

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:00.120
that users familiar with
Kubernetes kube control,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.210
it's very similar to that.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:02.752
It's sort of modeled on it.

00:18:02.752 --> 00:18:05.210
Basically, you do the same
thing you would with Kubernetes.

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:08.015
You write a file, and
then you run Istio Control

00:18:08.015 --> 00:18:09.390
and pass it the
name of the file.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:10.899
And it goes in and
configures Istio.

00:18:10.899 --> 00:18:13.190
And so that's how you'll do
things like set up a quota.

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:17.450
So you can get a file that says,
user A calling service B only

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:19.280
gets 100 QPS.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:23.310
That's maybe one or two
lines of configuration.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:24.370
You run Istio Control.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:25.996
And now that will
actually be enforced.

00:18:25.996 --> 00:18:26.911
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:26.911 --> 00:18:29.170
And with this, you really
get things like quota,

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:31.910
as we were talking, things
like retrying in case

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:33.560
the service is down.

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.040
What about the monitoring?

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:36.377
Is it a dashboard there?

00:18:36.377 --> 00:18:38.210
Does it integrate with
other services like--

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:39.980
I don't know-- like Stackdriver?

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:41.130
How does this work?

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:41.921
SVEN MAWSON: Right.

00:18:41.921 --> 00:18:44.390
So one of Istio's
philosophies is

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:47.390
that we don't want to build
things other people have built.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:50.000
So we didn't build any
monitoring ourselves, right?

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.050
We're using other
monitoring systems.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:55.359
The first one we
support is Prometheus.

00:18:55.359 --> 00:18:57.400
We've sort of gone with
out-of-the-box Prometheus

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.010
support, out-of-the-box a
Prometheus dashboard, actually,

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.614
that will automatically
show you all your services,

00:19:02.614 --> 00:19:05.030
and their traffic, and errors,
and a bunch of other useful

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:06.369
things.

00:19:06.369 --> 00:19:08.660
And there's sort of metrics
that we collect by default.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:12.680
And then you go and extend that
as a user and add more metrics,

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:14.120
add better dashboards,
add alerts,

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:14.960
whenever you want to do.

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:17.085
VARUN TALWAR: Just wnat to
add what this tier gives

00:19:17.085 --> 00:19:20.030
you is more traffic
signals, more L7

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:22.880
metrics into the systems
that you're already

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:23.880
using for monitoring.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:27.350
So back to our example,
A talking to B. Yes,

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:31.760
you're interested in how much
CPU utilization and network

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:34.310
bandwidths and resources
being used by NB.

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:38.420
But you're probably
more interested in what

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:39.810
is the latency from A to B?

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:42.350
What is the error
rate between A and B?

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:45.470
How many response
[INAUDIBLE] by percentage?

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:48.860
So the things at L7 that you're
interested in you sort of just

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:49.994
get out-of-the-box.

00:19:49.994 --> 00:19:52.160
MARK MANDEL: I'm actually
very specifically thinking

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.140
of that example where
I would probably

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:56.420
have had of some sort
of try catch error

00:19:56.420 --> 00:19:58.590
code on both sides
of my application

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.947
that would have caught
that error, either sending

00:20:00.947 --> 00:20:03.530
or receiving, and then sent that
up to some sort of monitoring

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.070
unit.

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:06.269
But now with Istio, I
can put this in between,

00:20:06.269 --> 00:20:08.060
and it's going to handle
that sort of thing

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.694
for me, which
sounds really cool.

00:20:09.694 --> 00:20:10.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.340
I just wanted to mention
that, for our listeners,

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:16.490
for more context on Prometheus,
we actually released an episode

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:19.960
with one of the founders of
the project, Julius Volz,

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:21.780
with episode 82.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:24.090
So if you want to know more
about it, go check it out.

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:25.280
MARK MANDEL: So I was going
to ask another question,

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:26.540
since we also have Varun here.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:27.870
I know there's some--

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:29.120
I don't want to call it magic.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:30.580
There's some special
bits and pieces in there

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.290
that are particular
that are for gRPC

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:34.405
as well that sort of
work out of the box.

00:20:34.405 --> 00:20:36.150
Can you talk about
some of those?

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:36.941
VARUN TALWAR: Yeah.

00:20:36.941 --> 00:20:40.030
So the approach
here is it should

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:43.840
work even if your services
are gRPC services.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.940
So Istio should
work the same way--

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:49.060
hopefully better because
you're getting the HTTP/2

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.070
advantages and so on.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:56.170
So the interesting thing
here is the proxy in Istio

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:57.610
understands gRPC.

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:03.520
So basically, you don't have
to do any transcoding from REST

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.420
to gRPC.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:09.130
You don't have to do that
heavy lifting because the proxy

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:10.240
understands them.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.840
So our goal here is
Istio as such works

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:16.870
with the gRPC services as well.

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:20.650
Of course, in future, we
can make it even better,

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:24.190
that you can have the
policies you apply

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:27.080
and, you know, security
principles you apply.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:30.850
They can work with two gRPC
services even without proxy.

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:32.200
So we are not there yet.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.900
But I think our goal
is to enable people

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:37.360
to have all these capabilities.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:44.080
If you use proxy in the middle
or if you just build with gRPC,

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:48.400
you still get a lot of the
connectivity resiliency

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:52.330
and similar kind of traffic
management and policies

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:53.650
that Istio supports.

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.470
MARK MANDEL: And won't
Istio also do gRPC

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:56.950
to REST conversion as well?

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:58.283
SVEN MAWSON: It will eventually.

00:21:58.283 --> 00:21:59.158
It doesn't quite yet.

00:21:59.158 --> 00:22:00.116
MARK MANDEL: Oh really?

00:22:00.116 --> 00:22:00.846
OK.

00:22:00.846 --> 00:22:02.220
SVEN MAWSON: It's
on our roadmap.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:02.830
So yes, it will.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:03.520
MARK MANDEL: I got excited.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:04.570
I was reading it and
I saw that, and I

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:05.470
was like, oh, that's cool.

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:05.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:07.001
That sounds really interesting.

00:22:07.001 --> 00:22:09.130
Actually, since we're
talking about the roadmap--

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:09.880
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.680
We're targetting
the next release.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:15.310
Yeah, that's actually a feature
that someone in the community

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:17.430
actually created a
gRPC gateway that

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:21.370
does that based on the sort
of standard annotations

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.110
that Google released.

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:25.960
And we've also released
a Cloud Endpoints

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:27.190
product that also does this.

00:22:27.190 --> 00:22:29.980
And we're very
committed to letting

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:31.720
people write gRPC
services and have

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:35.200
those serve people using gRPC,
but also people just using

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:35.700
HTTP.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:36.616
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:36.616 --> 00:22:40.030
So since we're talking about
the roadmap, I wonder--

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:42.280
before we talk
about the roadmap,

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:45.400
is Istio version 1.0 ready?

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.860
Is this something that I
should be running in production

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:48.850
not yet?

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:50.220
And if not, when?

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:52.877
SVEN MAWSON: The version
so far is actually 0.1.

00:22:52.877 --> 00:22:53.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:54.360
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's like
1.0, just the other way around.

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:56.550
It's fine.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:57.425
SVEN MAWSON: Exactly.

00:22:57.425 --> 00:22:59.990
The smallest number
we could come up with.

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.895
So we don't actually recommend
people use this on production.

00:23:02.895 --> 00:23:05.820
The proxy itself, the Envoy
proxy, is production ready.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.220
It's been in
production environments

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.190
for at least a year,
if not longer, at Lyft.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:12.890
But the other pieces are new.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.175
And so if people are
worried about it at all,

00:23:15.175 --> 00:23:16.800
they probably shouldn't
rely on it yet.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:21.340
We're furiously working on more
stability, more performance,

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.560
more reliability
of those components

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:26.092
to make sure that
the bar of quality

00:23:26.092 --> 00:23:27.550
is much higher on
the next release.

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:29.770
And that's where
we expect people

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:32.944
should sort of have no problems
with using this in production.

00:23:32.944 --> 00:23:34.360
That said, I think
there's already

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:37.180
people using this in production
and doing fine with it.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.530
So you know, buyer beware.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.210
But there are people doing that.

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:41.126
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:23:41.126 --> 00:23:42.910
So please do not use
this in production,

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.130
but even though some already do.

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:45.630
That's--

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:47.450
MARK MANDEL: But if
you are, make sure

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:49.210
you submit feedback so that
the product can get better.

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:50.330
SVEN MAWSON: Exactly.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:50.450
Yes.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:51.280
Keep the bugs coming.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:52.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
then the question

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:53.800
is, what is the roadmap?

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:55.120
When do you think these--

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.450
what are the next features
that you're working on?

00:23:57.450 --> 00:24:01.420
What should we expect in
the coming months, or maybe

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:01.920
next year?

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:02.669
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah.

00:24:02.669 --> 00:24:05.109
So we're actually-- we're
trying to release pretty often.

00:24:05.109 --> 00:24:07.525
So we're actually hoping to
get at least two more releases

00:24:07.525 --> 00:24:08.500
in this year.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:11.050
The next release--
so there's a lot

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:12.900
of stability and
performance and sort

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:15.210
of figuring things
out going into that.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.620
So we learned a lot
in the first release.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:19.450
A lot of it was sort
of duct taped together

00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:21.790
to try and just make all the
pieces work, and maybe not

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:25.610
necessarily the sort of
right long term design.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:27.860
We're trying to finalize,
for example, how

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:30.130
partners and vendors and
other interested parties

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:33.780
can add their own back ends
and their own futures to Istio

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.050
and do that very simply.

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:36.580
Today, it's a little bit
harder than we'd like.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:38.830
So we're doing a lot of
refactoring to clean that up.

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:41.270
Probably the biggest
item in the next release

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:45.930
is actually going to be support
for raw VMs outside Kubernetes,

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:48.785
being able to run this on just
bare VMs, which kind of means

00:24:48.785 --> 00:24:50.910
you can run it basically
anywhere, although it will

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:52.580
be more effort than Kubernetes.

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:53.910
Kubernetes is one line.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.600
Raw VMs will take
more because you

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:57.956
have to run all the different
parts yourself, right?

00:24:57.956 --> 00:24:58.422
MARK MANDEL: Mhm.

00:24:58.422 --> 00:25:00.505
SVEN MAWSON: So that's
probably the biggest piece.

00:25:00.505 --> 00:25:03.490
And then after that, hopefully
in the sort of third release,

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:05.320
what I really want
to see is what

00:25:05.320 --> 00:25:07.560
we call hybrid,
which is maybe I'm

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:10.240
running a couple different
clusters and some VMs,

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:12.460
and I want all those to be
sort of part of one mesh

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:14.210
and be able to communicate
with each other

00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:15.710
and have consistent
authentication

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:19.380
and security and all the
goodness that Istio brings.

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:22.120
That is actually a
very difficult task,

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:23.540
though we're
hopefully up for it.

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:25.240
So hopefully in
the third release,

00:25:25.240 --> 00:25:26.360
that will be coming out.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:27.180
MARK MANDEL: Very nice.

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:28.513
So actually, that's interesting.

00:25:28.513 --> 00:25:31.330
So you mentioned,
Sven, cross cluster.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:36.640
So does Istio work outside
in, or does it work--

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:38.050
if I have two
Kubernetes clusters

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:39.970
and I want to send
information between them,

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:41.290
I'm guessing the
monitoring is separate

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:42.730
and the aggregation is separate.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:46.500
But even if I have just
external people using my APIs,

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.719
that will still come in
through Istio otherwise.

00:25:48.719 --> 00:25:50.260
It's not just internal
to Kubernetes.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.060
SVEN MAWSON: Right.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:51.310
Yeah.

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:52.370
That's a great question.

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:56.060
So Istio does come with what's
called an ingress controller.

00:25:56.060 --> 00:25:58.346
So if you're just
using Kubernetes,

00:25:58.346 --> 00:26:00.150
Kubernetes has an
ingress resource

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:03.380
that you configure sort of how
things coming into the cluster

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:06.430
act, really at the
HTTP level only.

00:26:06.430 --> 00:26:09.420
And Istio has an
implementation of that

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:11.590
so that the Envoy
proxy basically

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:15.400
acts as that ingress proxy
and secures everything

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.220
once it hits that point.

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:22.300
Once things are into the Istio
world, into the Istio mesh,

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:24.230
everything gets secured.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:24.980
We have that part.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:28.390
What we don't have yet is sort
of those two clusters sort

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:30.760
of knowing about each other
and being able to do things

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:34.045
like global load balancing
across things and failover

00:26:34.045 --> 00:26:36.125
and other sort of--

00:26:36.125 --> 00:26:38.750
the really cool stuff you can do
once you know about everything

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:39.250
in both places.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:39.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:40.990
That sounds really interesting.

00:26:40.990 --> 00:26:43.000
Before we finish-- we're
kind of running out of time,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.041
but there is one question
that I said I would ask

00:26:45.041 --> 00:26:48.614
and I didn't ask yet, which
is, what does "Istio" mean?

00:26:48.614 --> 00:26:50.530
VARUN TALWAR: As you
know, we love Greek words

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:54.950
here at Google Cloud, and they
seem to do well in open source.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:27:00.830
So technically, I think
it's "sail" in Greek.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:03.910
Helmsman, Kubernetes, we're
sort of going with the analogy.

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:05.650
We're not just Kubernetes first.

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:08.050
We also take inspiration
from their naming.

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:11.350
So with that name,
there was another hope

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:14.300
that we would get ist.io.

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:17.200
But we figured that
wasn't available.

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:19.180
And we're soon going
to have stickers.

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:21.970
So it becomes a real project
when you have stickers.

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:23.470
MARK MANDEL: When
you have stickers.

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:24.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And t-shirts.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:24.760
I'm waiting for one.

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:24.820
MARK MANDEL: T-shirts.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:25.310
What about t-shirts?

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:26.190
Do we get t-shirts?

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:27.070
VARUN TALWAR: We have
t-shirts already.

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:28.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
then the web page

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.730
for those that would like to
learn more about the project

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:31.332
is istio.io.

00:27:31.332 --> 00:27:32.040
SVEN MAWSON: Yes.

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:35.270
Istio.io is the website.

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:38.050
We've tried-- I think there's
decent documentation on getting

00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:39.610
started and all that.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.443
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
I'm just looking at it,

00:27:41.443 --> 00:27:42.850
and it's adorable.

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:44.808
SVEN MAWSON: I was just
going to real quick say

00:27:44.808 --> 00:27:46.854
that on istio.io, there
is also a lot more

00:27:46.854 --> 00:27:48.520
of this sort of
philosophy behind Istio,

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:49.870
as well as just getting started.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:52.780
So you can learn more
about why we created it,

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.890
and how it works, and what
the driving philosophies are

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.480
behind it which is all, at least
i think, pretty interesting.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:27:58.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:01.180
Well, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:03.559
today to tell us a
little bit about Istio

00:28:03.559 --> 00:28:05.100
and get us excited
about the roadmap.

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:05.530
VARUN TALWAR: Awesome.

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:06.280
Thanks for having us.

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:06.670
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah.

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:07.750
It's great to be here.

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:10.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So thanks
again to Varun and Sven

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.120
to spend the time to
tell us a little bit more

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:15.019
about Istio, about the
goals and the roadmap,

00:28:15.019 --> 00:28:16.810
and a little bit of
the history of it, too.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:19.090
So very, very interesting
open source project.

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:21.030
And the question of
the week for today

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:23.210
is actually related
to the same topic.

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:25.480
It's about Istio and,
of course, Kubernetes.

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:28.300
And the question is
basically, I use Kubernetes.

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:28.870
I love it.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:31.840
And I'm already using it
with some applications.

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:34.010
My applications,
I have all the dud

00:28:34.010 --> 00:28:37.600
YAMLs to describe the
services and the paths and all

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:38.470
that stuff.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:40.720
And I want to use Istio.

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:41.560
How do I do this?

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:42.740
Like, what is the best way?

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:45.040
Is there, like,
maybe some tutorial

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:46.570
to explain how to do this?

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:47.320
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:48.790
So this is a really
good question.

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:51.084
So overarching, there is
actually a really great blog

00:28:51.084 --> 00:28:53.500
post on the Kubernetes blog--
we'll link to it in the show

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:54.000
notes--

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.800
"Managing Micro Services
With the Istio Service Mesh,"

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:59.920
where what it does is it
takes an existing book info

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:03.220
application and then goes
and applies Istio to it

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:06.010
so that you can see metrics
around ingress traffic

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:07.492
in and out and see
how that works.

00:29:07.492 --> 00:29:08.950
The thing that it
highlights, and I

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:10.900
think that's kind
of neat, is Istio

00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:12.634
comes with Istio CTL,
or Istio Control,

00:29:12.634 --> 00:29:14.800
depending on how you like
to pronounce those things.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:16.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Istio Cuddle.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:17.620
MARK MANDEL: Istio-- no, no.

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:18.880
Istio CTL.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:22.174
And one of the commands it has
is a thing called kube-inject.

00:29:22.174 --> 00:29:23.590
Basically, the
documentation reads

00:29:23.590 --> 00:29:26.830
that the Envoy sidecar that
Istio uses isn't automatically

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:29.680
injected with Istio,
at least not yet.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.510
It's a project in progress, so
they may get there eventually.

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:35.540
But instead, you can use
this kube-inject command

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:38.920
on the Istio CTL where you
can hand it a Kubernetes YAML,

00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:42.250
and it will change the
configuration for you.

00:29:42.250 --> 00:29:44.810
And basically, you can do that
as many times as you like.

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:46.930
And then, as the
project Istio changes,

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:49.150
which it probably will--
it's still in progress--

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:51.810
you can keep redoing
that to apply that Envoy

00:29:51.810 --> 00:29:55.130
sidecar in a canonical way
for Istio going forward.

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:56.980
So essentially, a really
nice little toolkit

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:58.582
showing how you can do things.

00:29:58.582 --> 00:30:00.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It is interesting.

